---
// firma2-page-header — the page's own top edge: the breadcrumb trail and the
// page title (plus whatever actions the page hangs off them), held at the top of
// the viewport while the rest of the page scrolls underneath.
//
// Slots: "actions" (the page's workflow, trailing the title), "toolbar"
// (record-level utilities, trailing the BREADCRUMB — crumbed pages only; see
// the note in the markup) and "status" (what STATE the record is in — a pill
// or two, under the title).
//
// WHY IT IS PINNED AND THE APP BAR IS NOT. AppLayout scrolls the DOCUMENT, so
// the bar with the omnibox and the account menu rides up and out of the way —
// it is chrome for the app, and once you are reading a record you are done with
// it. What you are never done with is WHICH record you are reading and the way
// back out of it, and on a long page (a project detail runs six sections) both
// scroll off within a screen. So the header is the one band that stays: the
// crumb is the exit, the title is the answer to "what am I looking at", and the
// actions are the thing you came to do.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: esa-page-header IS this header and remains the whole of the
// markup — the title role, the eyebrow, the breadcrumbs and actions slots are
// all its own, and esa-breadcrumbs renders the trail. Nothing here re-implements
// either. What is added is ONE behaviour neither lego has: the band. Walked the
// catalog (`ls node_modules/@esa/ecology/src/components/`) for a sticky/affix/
// page-top primitive — the only sticky-adjacent lego is esa-back-to-top, which
// is a floating button, not a region, and esa-app-bar is the app's bar, not the
// page's. Beacon is not cloned in this checkout. Every prototype opens with this
// section, so one shared component is its home rather than the same four rules
// pasted into four pages — which is also what keeps the band's height, its
// hairline and its bleed identical on every screen.
import EsaPageHeader from '@esa/ecology/esa-page-header.astro';
import EsaBreadcrumbs from '@esa/ecology/esa-breadcrumbs.astro';
import Firma2EditableField from './firma2-editable-field.astro';

/** One step of the trail. The LAST crumb is the current page and takes no href —
    esa-breadcrumbs marks it aria-current="page" on that basis. */
export interface Crumb {
  label: string;
  href?: string;
}

/** Makes the h1 a field. `field` is the record's own key ("projectName");
    `label` is the word the display's edit hint and its text box are announced with. */
export interface EditableTitle {
  field: string;
  label: string;
  /**
   * The STORED value when it differs from the displayed title — a record that
   * has no name yet shows its fallback ("Untitled measure") as the h1, and the
   * editor must open EMPTY rather than seeded with the fallback, or the
   * fallback becomes the name the first time anyone commits. Defaults to the
   * title itself, which is the named-record case.
   */
  editValue?: string;
}

interface Props {
  /** The page's name. Renders as the h1. */
  title: string;
  /** The trail above the title. Omit on a top-level page, where a single crumb
      would only repeat the title one line below it. */
  crumbs?: Crumb[];
  /**
   * Present when the title is a STORED VALUE the reader may correct — a record's
   * name, not a screen's name. Omit on every page whose title is a route.
   */
  editableTitle?: EditableTitle;
}

const { title, crumbs, editableTitle } = Astro.props;
const hasCrumbs = (crumbs?.length ?? 0) > 0;
const hasStatus = Astro.slots.has('status');
const hasToolbar = Astro.slots.has('toolbar');
---
{/* THE BAND IS A STACK so the status row below the title takes the same 8px
    step esa-page-header puts between its own eyebrow and its h1 — the status
    reads as part of the title block rather than as a second row of chrome. */}
<div class="firma2-page-header stack" data-gap="xs">
  {/* TWO BRANCHES, not one with a conditional inside. esa-page-header decides
      whether to render its breadcrumbs container with Astro.slots.has(), and a
      slot counts as FILLED as soon as an element carrying `slot="breadcrumbs"`
      appears in the children — even inside an expression that evaluates false.
      Passing `{hasCrumbs && <EsaBreadcrumbs …/>}` therefore rendered an empty
      breadcrumbs div on every top-level page, and its 12px bottom margin showed
      up as unexplained space over the title. The only way to leave the slot
      genuinely empty is not to write it, so the crumb-less page gets its own
      call. Actions forward unconditionally: an empty actions cluster is a
      zero-width flex box on the bar's trailing edge and changes nothing. */}
  {hasCrumbs ? (
    <EsaPageHeader title={title}>
      {/* THE CRUMB ROW IS ALSO THE TOOLBAR ROW. Two slots, two kinds of act:
          "actions" trails the TITLE and is for the page's workflow (the
          catalog's primary "New measure"); "toolbar" trails the CRUMB and is
          for record-level utilities — smaller, quieter, chrome rather than
          workflow (the project page's Share · Fact sheet · History). The
          crumb and the toolbar share one .repel because they are the same
          kind of thing: the strip above the title is the page's frame — the
          way out on its leading edge, the things you can do WITH the page on
          its trailing edge — and the title below stays the record's own.
          Requires crumbs: the toolbar rides the crumb row, and a top-level
          page (no crumbs) has no such row to ride — its actions belong
          beside the title. */}
      <div slot="breadcrumbs" class="repel" data-gap="md">
        <EsaBreadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
        {hasToolbar && (
          <div class="firma2-page-header__toolbar cluster" data-gap="xs">
            <slot name="toolbar" />
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
      <slot name="actions" slot="actions" />
    </EsaPageHeader>
  ) : (
    <EsaPageHeader title={title}>
      <slot name="actions" slot="actions" />
    </EsaPageHeader>
  )}

  {/* THE TITLE, AS A FIELD. It renders here, outside the header, and the script
      below moves it into the title block around the h1 esa-page-header already
      rendered — the lego takes its title as a string prop and offers no slot to
      put a field in, so adoption is the only composition available that does not
      print the name twice. Rendered as MARKUP by the shared component, never
      built in JS: the script moves nodes, it does not author any.

      Filed as a /request-lego — a `title` slot on esa-page-header would let this
      be plain composition, and every spoke with an editable record name wants it. */}
  {editableTitle && (
    <Firma2EditableField
      field={editableTitle.field}
      label={editableTitle.label}
      value={title}
      editValue={editableTitle.editValue}
      as="page-title"
      required
    />
  )}

  {/* THE RECORD'S STATUS, UNDER ITS NAME — not trailing it in the actions
      cluster, which is where it sat because that is the only slot
      esa-page-header offers beside the title. Two things were wrong with that
      seat. A stage pill is not an action: it sits in the one cluster whose
      every other occupant is a control, on the far side of a bar that can be
      1200px wide, so the fact it qualifies is separated from the name it
      qualifies by the whole width of the screen. Under the title it is read in
      the same glance as the name, in the reading order a record has anyway —
      what it is, then what state it is in.

      It renders here, after the header rather than inside the title column,
      because the lego has no slot there (the same gap the editable title works
      around, and the same /request-lego). Nothing has to move it: with the
      status out of the actions cluster the bar holds only the title, so the row
      directly under the header IS the row directly under the title, and it
      lands on the title's own left edge with no adoption script to fail.

      A .cluster, so several status pills — or a pill and a timestamp — sit in a
      row and wrap on a narrow window without this component learning anything
      about what the page put in it. */}
  {hasStatus && (
    <div class="firma2-page-header__status cluster" data-gap="xs">
      <slot name="status" />
    </div>
  )}
</div>

<script>
  // THE BAND'S HEIGHT IS PUBLISHED AS A CUSTOM PROPERTY. Any section sized
  // against the FOLD has to subtract whatever this band occupies above it, and
  // the band's height is a runtime fact — one line of title or two, crumb or no
  // crumb, taller again if a long title wraps at a narrow width. Writing it to
  // :root turns "how tall is the header" into something CSS can read, so a
  // full-bleed section states `calc(100dvh - var(--firma2-band-height) - …)` and
  // re-solves it on every resize instead of carrying a constant that is right at
  // one width. The project detail page's map is the first consumer. The
  // observer covers the wrap case, which is a live resize.
  //
  // The band used to pin to the top of the scroll and this script also set
  // scroll-padding on the root so focus never landed beneath it. It scrolls
  // with the page now (it covered the content passing under it), so nothing is
  // ever obscured and the scroll-padding went with the pinning.
  const band = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.firma2-page-header');
  if (band) {
    const sync = (): void => {
      const h = Math.round(band.getBoundingClientRect().height);
      document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--firma2-band-height', `${h}px`);
    };
    sync();
    new ResizeObserver(sync).observe(band);
  }

  // ── THE EDITABLE TITLE: dock the field around the lego's own h1 ─────────────
  //
  // esa-page-header takes its title as a STRING prop and renders the h1 itself,
  // so there is no slot a field can be composed into. The two alternatives were
  // both worse: rendering a second heading and hiding the lego's puts the record's
  // name in the DOM twice and ships two h1s to anyone whose JS never ran, and
  // re-implementing the header's bar in the spoke drifts the one component every
  // page opens with. So the field ADOPTS the heading — the h1 esa-page-header
  // rendered is moved into the row, and the row's own display BUTTON (which the
  // field server-renders carrying the same title text) is moved inside the h1.
  // The heading keeps its class and its scope hash, so it looks identical after
  // the move; its content is now the button, so the heading's accessible name is
  // still exactly the project name (a button inside a heading is valid HTML —
  // the reverse nesting is not).
  //
  // The row's markup is server-rendered by firma2-editable-field; this only moves
  // nodes, it never authors any. Everything after the move — hover chrome, open,
  // seed, commit-on-leave, Enter, Escape, focus return — is that component's
  // delegated script, unchanged and shared with every other field on the page.
  const titleField = band?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-editable-field][data-adopt-display]');
  const heading = band?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.esa-page-header__title');

  if (band && titleField && heading) {
    // Grab the button BEFORE any node moves — after replaceChildren it is the
    // h1's content, and querying for it then would still work, but taking the
    // reference first keeps the sequence order-proof.
    const display = titleField.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-field-display]');
    heading.replaceWith(titleField);
    titleField.prepend(heading);
    if (display) heading.replaceChildren(display);
    titleField.hidden = false;

    // THE EDITOR KEEPS THE TITLE'S TYPE. Left alone, opening the title swaps a
    // 30px two-line heading for a 40px box of 15px text — the record's name
    // shrinks to half size at the exact moment someone is working on it, and
    // the band collapses around it. The input takes the h1's own
    // COMPUTED type instead (measured, not restated — the heading's composite
    // class stays the single source of truth), and the control's box goes
    // height:auto so it fits what it now holds.
    //
    // An adopted stylesheet into the lego's shadow root — the same technique,
    // and the same justification, as AppLayout's CONTROL_PINS and the select
    // option alignment in firma2-editable-field: a hub gap (no size rung
    // renders heading-scale text), worth a /request-lego, escaped locally and
    // loudly. `:host`-prefixed so it outranks the CONTROL_PINS 40px pin
    // regardless of which sheet lands last.
    const titleInput = titleField.querySelector<HTMLElement>('esa-text-field');
    if (titleInput) {
      customElements.whenDefined('esa-text-field').then(async () => {
        await (titleInput as HTMLElement & { updateComplete?: Promise<unknown> }).updateComplete;
        const root = titleInput.shadowRoot;
        if (!root) return;
        const type = getComputedStyle(heading);
        const sheet = new CSSStyleSheet();
        sheet.replaceSync(`
          :host .control { height: auto; }
          :host .input {
            font-size: ${type.fontSize};
            font-weight: ${type.fontWeight};
            line-height: ${type.lineHeight};
            letter-spacing: ${type.letterSpacing};
          }
        `);
        root.adoptedStyleSheets = [...root.adoptedStyleSheets, sheet];
      });
    }

    // THE NAME IS ON SCREEN TWICE, and the second one is one line up. The last
    // breadcrumb IS the current page, so a rename that left it reading the old
    // name would show a reader two names for one record with 12px between them.
    // The browser tab is the third copy and gets the same treatment: it is how a
    // reader picks this record out of eight open tabs.
    const crumb = band.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.esa-breadcrumbs__current');
    let previous = heading.textContent?.trim() ?? '';

    titleField.addEventListener('firma2:field-save', (event: Event) => {
      const next = (event as CustomEvent<{ value: string }>).detail?.value?.trim() ?? '';
      if (!next || next === previous) return;
      if (crumb) crumb.textContent = next;
      // Replaced rather than rebuilt: the tab title is `<name> — <app>`, and the
      // suffix is the page's to compose, not this component's to know.
      if (previous) document.title = document.title.replace(previous, next);
      previous = next;
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* THE BAND SCROLLS WITH THE PAGE. It was `position: sticky` at --z-header
     until 2026-09-11, and it came off at the author's direction: pinned, it
     sat over whatever content was passing beneath it. The band is the page's
     own top, so it leaves with the page. */
  .firma2-page-header {
    /* FULL BLEED, then pad back. esa-app-shell's content pane carries
       `padding: var(--spacing-500)`, so a band that respected it would stop
       short of the rail and the right edge and read as a floating card rather
       than as the top of the page. The negative margins reach through the
       gutters, the padding puts the title back on the content column's own
       left edge, and the negative block-start lets the band sit against the app
       bar with no seam of canvas between them.

       This is the one place in the spoke that knows the pane's padding value.
       It is the shell's own literal (esa-app-shell.astro), not a token the pane
       exposes — worth a /request-lego for a `--app-shell-content-padding` hook
       so a full-bleed child can read it instead of restating it.

       THE TRAILING BLEED IS A HOOK, the leading one is not. A page whose content
       runs to the window's right edge wants both, and that is the default. A
       page with something ELSE on that edge — the project detail page pins a
       full-height record rail there — wants the band to stop at the column it
       belongs to, or 24px of bleed is 24px of band reaching into the rail's
       top corner. Set `--firma2-band-bleed-end: 0px` on
       any ancestor to close it. There is no matching hook on the leading edge
       because nothing has ever needed one — the rail is on the other side and
       the app's nav is outside the pane entirely. */
    --_bleed: var(--spacing-500, 1.5rem);
    margin-inline-start: calc(var(--_bleed) * -1);
    margin-inline-end: calc(var(--firma2-band-bleed-end, var(--_bleed)) * -1);
    margin-block-start: calc(var(--_bleed) * -1);
    padding-inline-start: var(--_bleed);
    padding-inline-end: var(--firma2-band-bleed-end, var(--_bleed));
    padding-block: var(--spacing-400, 1rem);

    /* The CANVAS role — the same surface the pane is painted with, so the band
       is not a second elevation stacked on the page but the page's own top.

       NO HAIRLINE. There was a 1px rule along the bottom edge; it read as the
       rule under a page title, and the project's description sits directly
       under the band, so a line drawn between a record's name and the sentence
       saying what that record IS separated two halves of one thought. Removed
       2026-08-20 at the author's direction. */
    background: var(--color-background-default, #fcfcfc);
  }

  /* THE ACTIONS SIT ON THE TITLE'S CENTRE LINE. The lego top-aligns its bar,
     which is right for a title that wraps beside a stack of buttons; every
     title on this site is one line, so a control a few pixels shorter than the
     h1 hung off its top edge instead of centring on it. The setup hub's meter
     (a sentence and a row of marks) was the first to show it at 28px against a
     40px line. Same :global() reasoning as the column rule below. */
  .firma2-page-header :global(.esa-page-header__actions) {
    align-self: center;
  }

  /* THE TITLE COLUMN TAKES THE BAR'S SLACK. ALWAYS — not, as it did until now,
     only for the duration of an edit.

     esa-page-header sizes that column to its content, and shrink-to-fit is a
     defensible default for a heading that sits beside actions and stops. It is
     wrong here for a measurable reason: the adopted title is not a bare string
     any more, it is a BUTTON around the string, and that button carries
     `padding-inline` so the hover plate extends past the glyphs. Shrink-to-fit
     sizes the column from the text's max-content and the padding then eats into
     it from both sides, so the line box comes out ~15px SHORT of the very text
     it was sized for — and the heading wraps with two thirds of the bar empty
     beside it. "Bear River Gravel Augmentation" needs 452px, had 1184px of bar
     to sit in, and was given 437px. `text-wrap: balance` on the heading role is
     what makes the result look deliberate rather than broken: it splits the
     overflow into two even lines, so the title reads as a two-line design
     decision instead of as a box 15px too small.

     Growing the column costs nothing anywhere: the actions keep their width
     because the lego declares `flex-shrink: 0` on them, and `min-width: 0` on
     the column (also the lego's) means a genuinely long title still yields
     rather than pushing them off the edge. A title that wraps after this is a
     title that really is wider than the bar.

     The edit state no longer needs a rule of its own — it was the same
     declaration, for the same reason (a text box sized to a heading's content
     collapsed to ~240px), and it is now simply the resting behaviour.

     :global() because the column belongs to esa-page-header and carries its
     scope hash; contained by .firma2-page-header, so no other header on any page
     can be reached.

     BASIS 0, NOT auto, and the difference only shows once the actions cluster
     has occupants again (the project page's record actions were the first).
     The lego's bar is flex-wrap: wrap, and wrap happens at flex-BASIS: with
     `auto`, a title whose content width plus the actions' width overflows the
     bar wraps the whole cluster onto a second row — buttons stacked under the
     h1, above the status pill — before the title's min-width: 0 ever gets the
     chance to let it shrink. At basis 0 the line is laid out from the actions'
     width alone, so the cluster keeps the first row's trailing edge and the
     title takes what remains, balance-wrapping onto two lines when the name
     really is wider than its share. On a page with no actions nothing changes:
     the column still grows to the full bar. */
  .firma2-page-header :global(.esa-page-header__titles) {
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }
</style>
